+# Decision: Change Delivery Transport
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard maintains one authoritative board per event, with editors submitting commands over HTTPS and up to 800 staff browsers displaying the live board across 40 weekend markets. During opening-time bursts of up to 20 edits per second, browsers must reflect committed changes within 15 seconds and surface connectivity failures rather than showing a stale board as current.
+
+The choice of transport for delivering committed changes from the backend to connected browsers shapes three downstream subsystems together: how the change store indexes and publishes revisions, whether the delivery tier is stateless or connection-stateful, and how browsers subscribe, reconnect, and catch up after a drop. All three candidate transports can satisfy the 15-second freshness goal and preserve a single authoritative event revision; the decision turns on how latency, operational burden, and vendor coupling are traded.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Vendor independence and predictable cost** *(major — informed by the tenet permitting self-hosted infrastructure for non-customer operational data)*: Avoiding recurring per-connection or per-message realtime vendor fees and avoiding coupling core board delivery to an external provider's subscription and publication model.
+- **Update immediacy** *(moderate)*: Pushing committed changes to browsers as they commit rather than waiting up to a polling interval, improving coordination during opening-time bursts even though subsecond latency is not strictly required by the 15-second goal.
+- **Operational simplicity** *(major)*: Avoiding stateful connection capacity planning, reconnect-storm handling, and in-house pub/sub fan-out operations.
+- **Downstream subsystem coupling** *(minor)*: Keeping change storage, delivery, and browser recovery loosely coupled with minimal cross-component machinery (outboxes, session state, or provider reconciliation).
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+- **Description**: Browsers periodically issue stateless HTTPS requests every five seconds with their last seen revision cursor, and the delivery tier queries the change store for any revisions after that cursor. No persistent connections or server-side push sessions are maintained.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - **Vendor independence and predictable cost**: **strong** — Uses standard HTTPS infrastructure with no external realtime provider or usage-based realtime billing.
+  - **Update immediacy**: **partial** — Meets the 15-second freshness goal, but introduces up to five seconds of normal lag and relies on repeated read traffic during idle periods.
+  - **Operational simplicity**: **strong** — Delivery remains stateless request/response; horizontal scaling, load balancing, and browser recovery require no long-lived connection state.
+  - **Downstream subsystem coupling**: **strong** — Change store needs only revision cursor queries, delivery needs no session state, and browsers run a simple polling loop.
+- **Summary**: Simplest stateless operation and lowest coupling, at the cost of multi-second lag and repeated polling reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+- **Description**: The delivery tier terminates persistent WebSocket connections from browsers, authenticates subscriptions, and fans out committed revisions published by the change store. Browsers maintain a connection, surface disconnects visibly, and request missed revisions on reconnect.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - **Vendor independence and predictable cost**: **strong** — Runs entirely on self-hosted infrastructure with no external realtime vendor fees or provider lock-in.
+  - **Update immediacy**: **strong** — Pushes committed changes immediately on publication, providing subsecond updates during opening-time bursts.
+  - **Operational simplicity**: **weak** — Requires owning WebSocket connection capacity (up to 800 simultaneous browsers), graceful server restarts, and reconnect/catch-up storms.
+  - **Downstream subsystem coupling**: **partial** — Requires the change store to support replayable revisions and reliable publication to delivery, delivery to manage stateful sessions, and browsers to coordinate live stream state with catch-up queries.
+- **Summary**: Fast push updates without a realtime vendor bill, at the cost of owning stateful connection capacity and reconnect/catch-up complexity.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+- **Description**: The change store or backend publishes committed revisions to a managed third-party realtime service, and browsers subscribe directly to that provider's channels for live updates while falling back to the backend for missed-revision recovery.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - **Vendor independence and predictable cost**: **weak** — Introduces recurring vendor fees and couples delivery and browser clients to the provider's connection, auth, and channel semantics.
+  - **Update immediacy**: **strong** — Delivers pushed updates with low latency as soon as revisions are published to the provider.
+  - **Operational simplicity**: **partial** — Offloads WebSocket connection scaling and fan-out operations, though the system must still manage provider credentials, channel authorization, and missed-publication reconciliation.
+  - **Downstream subsystem coupling**: **partial** — Change store needs reliable publication reconciliation with the external provider, delivery wraps provider tokens/channels, and browsers depend on the provider's SDK and subscription model.
+- **Summary**: Fast updates with offloaded connection scaling, at the cost of recurring vendor fees, provider coupling, and external publication reconciliation.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is selected.
+
+It wins on the combination of **vendor independence and predictable cost** *(major)* and **update immediacy** *(moderate)*: MarketBoard's updates contain only stall IDs and operational statuses, the tenet permitting self-hosted infrastructure applies directly, and the pilot scale of 800 simultaneous browsers and 20 edits per second is small enough to run in-house without a managed realtime provider. Five-second polling was rejected because multi-second lag and constant read polling are unnecessary when self-hosted sockets are feasible; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+This selection intentionally trades away **operational simplicity** *(major)* and imposes concrete obligations on the three downstream design nodes:
+- **Change store**: Must persist replayable event revisions and provide reliable publication so delivery does not miss committed edits.
+- **Delivery**: Must manage WebSocket session lifecycle, subscription authorization, and live fan-out for up to 800 concurrent browsers.
+- **Browser sync**: Must handle WebSocket reconnects, revision catch-up after disconnects, and visible connectivity-failure states per the connectivity tenet.
